  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nAn upper level trough centered over Canada extends southward over \nthe Midwest and Northeast. An upper ridge is centered over Arizona \nand New Mexico and extends northward to Montana. Nebraska and Kansas \nare in between these two features. Winds across south central and \ncentral Nebraska and north central Kansas are mostly out of the \nsouth to southeast at 5 to 15 MPH with gusts up to 15 to 20 MPH \nacross mainly western portions of the area. High temperatures today \nwill be in the 80s with mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies. Some \nstorms may impact (20% or less chance) mainly western portions of \nthe area (west of the Tri-Cities) late this afternoon into this \nevening. If these storms develop, they could become strong to severe \ngiven fairly high CAPE, wind shear, and lapse rates. Perhaps the \ndeciding factor in storm development will be if a shortwave trough \nmoves over/near the area. Hail up to around the size of half dollars \nand wind gusts up to around 60 MPH will be the main threats with \nthese storms. Additional storm development is more likely (up to 35% \nchance) later tonight into Friday morning. This development is most \nlikely along and north and east of the Tri-Cities area. These \nadditional storms are not expected to be severe but some strong \nstorms with small hail and wind gusts up to 50 MPH are not out of \nthe question. Low temperatures tonight will be in the 60s. \n\nShowers and storms may linger into the morning hours on Friday from \nHighway 281 eastward. Winds across the area will strengthen out of \nthe south Friday afternoon at around 15 to 20 MPH with gusts up to \n25 to 35 MPH. Temperatures Friday afternoon will warm up into the \nmid 80s to mid 90s with sunny to mostly sunny skies. Showers and \nthunderstorms may move into the area (25% or less chance) Friday \nevening and night from the west and northwest. Some of these storms, \nif they develop and move into the area, may become strong to severe. \nThe earlier the storms develop, the more likely they will be severe \ndue to decreasing CAPE and wind shear during the evening and night \nhours. Large hail and damaging winds will be the main threats with \nthese storms. Again, the determining factor on severe storms (and \ntiming) may be when a shortwave moves into the area. Low \ntemperatures Friday night are expected to be warmer with cloudier \nskies and stronger winds. Lows are expected to mainly be in the 70s. \n\nThe upper ridge shifts further east this weekend which will result \nin warmer and drier conditions for south central and central \nNebraska and north central Kansas. The upper ridge becomes more \ncentered over the southeastern part of the country next week which \nwill allow for more upper disturbances to move over Nebraska and \nKansas. This will result in more opportunities for showers and \nthunderstorms for various portions of the area Monday through \nWednesday. High temperatures Saturday and Sunday are expected to be \nin the low 90s to low triple digits. Heat index values may reach \nnear 104 or 105 degrees in portions of north central Kansas and \nperhaps even in the southern row of Nebraska counties this weekend. \nTemperatures will decrease slightly Monday through Wednesday to the \nmid 80s to upper 90s.",
